  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B022",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B061",
    "B041"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks are a mature physical measurement regime founded on the specific magic-wavelength solution to a central many-atom spectroscopy problem. Katori's conceptual invention and Ye's decisive independent experimental development form a compact, complementary attribution. Internationally reproducible clock performance demonstrates that this is not an isolated technical achievement: it has durably extended precision comparison of time, frequency, and gravitational potential. The prize should recognize that verified transformation of measurement, without making its case depend on a future downstream discovery.",
    "case_against": "The historical record must still distinguish the original lattice-clock concept and defining experimental development from the many enabling contributions to optical clocks more generally. The citation should therefore remain confined to optical lattice clocks and should not claim all later optical-clock advances, nor overstate developing applications in fundamental physics and geodesy.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson prefers cosmic inflation because of its foundational account of primordial structure, while acknowledging that no distinctive observation uniquely selects inflation over all viable alternatives.",
        "response": "I retain great respect for B033, but that acknowledged lack of a discriminating signature is material when comparing it with B022. The lattice-clock achievement can be tested directly through repeatable performance and comparison across laboratories. Its central physical advance is bounded and established, rather than dependent on choosing among unresolved early-universe realizations."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson argues that spherical-aberration correction transformed TEM into an atom-resolving quantitative instrument, but calls for documentary reconstruction of the respective roles of Rose, Haider, Urban, and later developers.",
        "response": "I continue to regard B061 as a formidable materials-physics alternative; its impact on what can be measured in real materials is beyond dispute. But the requested reconstruction concerns the core theory-to-practical-corrector chain and hence the proposed allocation itself. B022 also requires historical review, yet its two-person conceptual and experimental core is presently more sharply delimited."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors conformal field theory because of its durable foundational influence and coherent three-person attribution, while noting its less immediate relation to direct measurement.",
        "response": "B027 is an exceptionally elegant and mature theoretical alternative. I nevertheless give priority to B022 because it combines a precise physical principle with a reproducible experimental regime whose agreement and control are themselves substantive evidence. The lattice-clock citation can recognize a bounded achievement without reducing it to mere engineering or relying on speculative applications."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The documentary priority record should establish the exact boundary between Katori's magic-wavelength lattice-clock concept, Ye's essential experimental development, and other enabling work in the broader optical-clock community.",
      "Whether citation language should mention precision measurement alone or also the already demonstrated reach to gravity and fundamental tests, while avoiding claims about applications that remain developing."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile; I have no identified collaboration or personal connection with either proposed laureate."
  }
}
